    "innovation": "Using large-ensemble climate model simulations, this paper reveals that Arctic warming amplification is stronger in a low-emission scenario compared to a high-emission one, because the persistence of sea ice allows the ice-albedo feedback to continue operating long-term.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic warming amplification",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "n future climate projections, as estimated by global climate models (GCMs) with increasing greenhouse gases, surface and lower-tropospheric temperatures over the Arctic region increase at a faster rate than the global average. This phenomenon is known as Arctic warming amplification (AA)1,2, which is a robust feature in GCMs3,4 and has also been identified in observational data for past decades 5,6. AA is only a recent emergence7, and the Arctic has warmed approximately four times as fast as the globes.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic warming amplification",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "mid-latitude",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Many studies also have reported that AA affects the atmospheric circulation not only over the regional scale9,10 but also over the hemispheric scale11,12 and is linked to mid-latitude extreme weather in both summer13 and winter14\u201316. In contrast, recent studies suggest that the link between AA and mid-latitude has become weaker17,18.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
